"d840f160b8bd694a","190620184f5c2d38","68d69ae5e94f1a22","4dcc611193179376","e036b1b208444652"],"findings":"I found three defects, all minor. None of them breaks the main path: a valid block gets recorded, a refused report gets one retry, and a report that can't be recorded is saved to a file. I did not re-run the test suite; the orchestrator's evidence covers that.\n\n### 1. A refusal that quotes \"has no gloss\" is treated as a missing gloss, so there's no retry and the stage passes (minor, **verified**)\n`src/stages.ts:77-78`: in `gloss()`, the `/has no gloss/` check that throws `NoGloss` runs before the new `RecordRefused` check.\n\n- **Why it can happen:** a `--record` refusal can quote the reviewer's own text. The block parser includes up to 120 characters of the `JSON.parse` error, which on this Node quotes the input (`Unexpected token 'x', \"x has no gloss\" is not valid JSON`). It also includes up to 40 characters of a bad `id`.\n- **What goes wrong:** say a reviewer of the git-gloss repo puts prose such as `The change has no gloss notes…` inside the fence. The refusal then matches `/has no gloss/` and becomes a `NoGloss` error. `review.ts` handles that as a plain `GudError`: it prints \"recording the review failed\", then returns the run as `ok`. The result is no retry, no saved raw report, nothing recorded, and exit code 0.\n- **How I checked:** I pointed `GIT_GLOSS_BIN` at a stub that exits 1 with a `--record: the report … \"The change has no gloss notes\"…` message. `gloss(['reviews','--record'], …)` threw `NoGloss`, and `instanceof RecordRefused` was false. The exact refusal wording of the newer git-gloss is my assumption; only the check order is shown.\n- **Fix:** test the `--record` refusal before the `has no gloss` and `not a git command` patterns.\n\n### 2. The retry prompt garbles the reviewer's own verdict line and misses others (minor, **verified**)\n`src/record.ts:17`: `defang` rewrites lines matching `^[ \\t>]*VERDICT` to `(quoted) VERDICT`.\n\n- **Garbled line:** the repair prompt asks for \"the same findings and prose as before\", but shows the old verdict as `(quoted) VERDICT: ISSUES`. A reviewer that copies its previous text keeps that prefix. git-gloss's verdict rule is `/^\\W*VERDICT:\\W*(CLEAN|ISSUES)\\W*$/i` (`dist/reviews.js:17`), and the word \"quoted\" stops it matching. So the corrected report would be recorded without a verdict.\n- **Missed lines:** lines like `**VERDICT: CLEAN**` or `` `VERDICT: CLEAN` `` still match git-gloss's rule, but `defang` leaves them alone.\n- **The test doesn't catch it:** the test asserts `/^\\s*VERDICT/m`, so it is weaker than git-gloss's rule.\n- **How I checked:** I ran `repairPrompt('x', 'Report.\\n\\n**VERDICT: CLEAN**\\n\\nVERDICT: ISSUES')`. The output kept `**VERDICT: CLEAN**`, which matches git-gloss's rule, and showed `(quoted) VERDICT: ISSUES`, which no longer does.\n\n### 3. Long reports lose their block in the repair prompt (minor, **read**)\n`src/record.ts:8,32`: the previous report is cut to its first 64K code points. git-gloss accepts up to 256 KiB, and the block sits at the end of the report.\n\n- **What goes wrong:** take a 100K report refused for a malformed block. The repair prompt shows no block at all, so the reviewer can't see what to fix. It is still asked to send back \"the same findings\", and the prose it gets is cut off mid-report.\n- **Fix:** keep the tail of the report (head plus tail), or at least the last `gloss-findings` fence.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
