e036b1b208444652"],"findings":"## Review of the structured-findings commit\n\n**Mismatches**\n\n1. **`headAndTail` produces a negative cut count and duplicated text when the findings fence sits near the start of a long report.** `src/record.ts` (`headAndTail`). When `fenceAt` is smaller than `HEAD` and the block is not in the tail region, `start = fenceAt`, so `gap = start - HEAD` goes negative and the tail overlaps the head. I ran `headAndTail` on a 100K report whose fence is at about character 6: the output says `[cut: -20474 characters from the middle]` and the fence appears twice. The author's claim that the head is kept and the cut is \"from the middle\" is false for this input. The tests do not cover a fence inside the head. **verified**\n\n2. **The README and the spec text describe the enforcement as in force, but the declared dependency cannot enforce it.** `docs` (README \"Structured findings (mandatory…)\") says every report ends with the block and that `git gloss reviews --open` works. `package.json` pins `@ok-ggwp/git-gloss` at `^0.7.0`, and the installed copy reports 0.6.0. Without `GIT_GLOSS_BIN` pointing at a 0.8+ build, `git gloss --record` accepts reports with no block, so no retry fires and no `--open` view exists. The TODO records this, but the README heading only partly hedges it, and the claim is in present tense. **verified** (version check) / **read** (README wording)\n\n3. **The spec-matching test does not check against git-gloss.** `tests` (`the block spec text matches the copy of git-gloss's spec…`). The first assertion compares `BLOCK_SPEC` with `test/fixtures/block-spec.txt`, and the author wrote both from the same source. The comparison with git-gloss runs only when `GIT_GLOSS_BIN` is set to a 0.8+ build, and none is available here. The group's \"evidence\" line overstates what is checked. **read**\n\n4. **The `detail` cap may not match the design.** `spec-prompts` (`BLOCK_SPEC`) says `detail` is at most 500, while `st-design.md` says `detail` is at most 2000. Either the design or the prompt is stale, and I could not check git-gloss's own `BLOCK_SPEC` to tell which. The copy in git-gud is the one the model sees, so a mismatch there sends reviewers the wrong limit. **read**\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n5. **The retry runs in the same clone the first attempt used.** `review-flow`. For `adversarial` and `mutate`, the first attempt may have left edited or unrestored files. The retry then runs on that state, and nothing checks or restores it first. **read**\n\n6. **`honestyAddendum` can duplicate the spec.** `spec-prompts`. The check `prompt.includes(BLOCK_SPEC)` is an exact match. If git-gloss 0.8's honesty prompt has even a small wording difference, the spec is inserted a second time. This is minor, and it does not show up until 0.8 is installed. **read**\n\n7. **The \"retry returned nothing\" signal uses an ad hoc `empty` property.** It is already in the TODO, so this is a note only. **read**\n\nAccurate and sound as stated: the retry ordering, the `RecordRefused` check placed before the other `gloss` error branches, the 0600 mode and the private temp directory from `saveRawReport`, and the verdict-line stripping in `withoutVerdicts`. I did not run the full suite, and I did not check the mutation claims in the evidence record.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
